Looking at this problem carefully:

**Erdős Problem 274** is the **Herzog–Schönheim conjecture**: if a group $G$ admits an exact covering by $k > 1$ left cosets $g_1H_1, \dots, g_kH_k$ of subgroups $H_1, \dots, H_k$, then two of the subgroups must have equal cardinality (equivalently, equal index $[G:H_i]$).

The theorem `erdos_274` is annotated `@[category research open]` — this is a genuinely **open research conjecture** in mathematics. The general (non-abelian) case has not been resolved by the mathematical community. Only the abelian case (`erdos_274.variants.abelian`) is marked `solved`, and even that is stated with `sorry` in this file (with a reference to an external formal proof repository).

The conjecture has been open since the 1970s. It has been verified in numerous special cases (finite groups of small order, groups satisfying certain index conditions, the case where all indices are bounded, etc.), but no general proof exists. The problem statement itself does not leak any solution — it only states the conjecture and references the literature.

As an open research problem at the frontier of group theory/combinatorics, I cannot produce a complete proof. Producing one would constitute a major mathematical breakthrough resolving the Herzog–Schönheim conjecture, which is beyond what I can honestly deliver.

